# Key Design Choices

This project inherits a few important design choices from the ABLE Workflow
template suite. The short version is below. For the full rationale behind these
choices, see [Features in the template docs]({% raw %}{{ able_workflow_copier_docs }}{% endraw %}/overview/features/).

## Workflow orchestration

The workflow layer uses **Snakemake** so each step can declare its inputs, outputs, dependencies, and execution environment explicitly. That makes the project easier to rerun, debug, and extend than a loose collection of scripts.

## Reusable package code

The Python package lives next to the workflow so reusable logic, schemas, extraction helpers, and tests can evolve with the workflow code that depends on them.

## Durable interfaces

This project leans toward explicit schemas, extraction helpers, and durable file formats so downstream code depends on documented interfaces rather than fragile file internals.

## Isolated environments

Dependencies are intended to stay close to the workflow steps that need them. That reduces hidden coupling and makes it easier to understand what each part of the project requires.

## Reviewable engineering defaults

Tests, typing, linting, docs, and CI are part of the intended project structure from the start. They are there to make changes easier to review and regressions easier to catch.
